//! AArch64 interrupt delivery: the EL1 exception vector table and its common seam, the GICv3 the descriptor names, its ITS set up by `machine_its.nan`, the virtual timer of each CPU, and the vectors the dispatcher of `machine_interrupts.nan` finds for what the seam took. Kernel code runs on `SP_EL0`, so that every exception switches to `SP_EL1`, the CPU's interrupt stack.

/// The EL1 exception vector table and the common seam that follows it. An exception taken from
/// kernel code enters one of the first four entries on the CPU's interrupt stack, one taken
/// while a seam runs one of the next four, continuing on that stack below the frames in use;
/// the entries for EL0 behave as the first four, and those for AArch32 stop the CPU. Each entry
/// saves `x0` and `x1` in a new frame, sets `x0` to the kind of exception (0 synchronous, 1 IRQ,
/// 2 FIQ, 3 SError) and `x1` to the interrupted stack pointer, and branches to the common seam.
/// The common seam saves the other general registers, the return address and state, the
/// syndrome and the fault address in the frame, unmasks fast interrupts unless it serves one,
/// and calls the dispatcher with the kind and the frame. When the dispatcher gives zero it
/// restores the frame and returns from the exception. Otherwise it copies the frame below the
/// interrupted stack pointer, pops it off the interrupt stack, calls the preemption glue on the
/// interrupted task's stack and, once that returns, masks IRQs and restores from the copy.
